[2026-09-13 07:10:13] REQ 260913064428-280 RESPONSE http=200 body_tail={"model":"glm-5.3-flash","created_at":"2026-09-13T07:10:13.413631169Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":"","tool_calls":[{"id":"call_m3xx2t7k","function":{"index":0,"name":"append_file","arguments":{"content":"* 2026-09-13 cycle 289 (~06:44-07:10 UTC): the alarm that wasn't, and the census that closed\n\nThe dropbear burst got re-attributed, and the correction is more\ninstructive than the original. c287 had pinned it on c282's census.\nWrong cycle. The burst was c288's inner-ssh -- a nested ssh whose\nouter hop carried BatchMode=yes and whose inner hop carried nothing.\nDropbear rejected the key, the inner ssh sent three empty passwords,\nand the alarm manufactured itself one hop deeper than the law\nlooked. The tool result sits in REQUESTS.log stamped 03:39:49 --\n\"Permission denied (publickey,password)\" -- the exact 3-fail shape\ndropbear logged at 03:39:48-49. Sixth own-tooling attribution. The\nlaw is now every-hop, not outer-hop.\n\nThe first-pass error is the part worth keeping. I read session\n260913033801 as c288 by matching it against LAST-CYCLE's end time,\nconcluded the burst fell in a gap where \"nothing aria was running,\"\nand nearly filed it as a real external event. What was actually\nwrong: REQUESTS.log stamps are UTC, the rotate journal is local, and\nthe session ID was minted 3h00m03s (exactly three hours) before the\nrotate line I was comparing it to. Law 50 again -- verify the CLOCK\n-- applied this time to my own logs. The pcap \"absence\" pointed the\nsame wrong way: sophon's enp10s0 capture showed zero .69-\u003e.103\ntraffic, but the capture's filter and vantage never covered sophon's\nown outbound unicast, and sophon's sshd journal has a silent gap\ncovering the whole window. Three instruments, three different ways\nof saying \"nothing happened,\" and the camera-side log plus the\ntool-result stamp were the only witnesses that told the truth.\n\nThe boot-activation watch closed clean. ext5 up 7742s with syslogd\n-R live, int2 up 8 minutes with it live, int3 still on its previous\nboot with the flag already set -- which is the better evidence, since\nit shows the flag persists across reboots rather than being injected\nper-boot. Seven of seven cron cameras boot into the sink. The\nstaircase mechanism is now closed at both ends: the reboots fire on\nschedule, every boot re-activates remote logging, and the splice\nsignature follows each one. Nothing left to watch in that class.\n\nOne transient to hold loosely: fleet-check read ext3 as 3/3 audio-dead\nat 07:01Z; sixty seconds later the same segments and the live RTSP\nstream were healthy. I reproduced nothing, the stream is fine, and I\nam deliberately not chasing it -- but if the 3/3-dead shape recurs\nwithout a real deafness behind it, that's a new false-alarm class in\nthe ear check and it will need a discriminator.\n\nThe sweep did not fire on its predicted ~07:05Z cadence, which\nconfirms again that .58's client is event-driven, not clocked. The\nARP logger is re-armed for two more hours. What I'd want next cycle:\nany recurrence logged with its trigger event, and -- if Nacho answers\n0062 -- this whole class either closes as someone's noisy app or\nopens into the question of what else that machine does.\n\nNext: sweep recurrence watch, relay 0060/0062 wait on Nacho, ext4\nstill his hands. The house is quiet in the way that means the\ninstruments are working.","filepath":"/root/personalization/audit/iar/aria/JOURNAL.org"}}}]},"done":false}\n{"model":"glm-5.3-flash","created_at":"2026-09-13T07:10:13.414142196Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":""},"done":false}\n{"model":"glm-5.3-flash","created_at":"2026-09-13T07:10:13.518960983Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":""},"done":true,"done_reason":"stop","total_duration":9347726100,"prompt_eval_count":163353,"prompt_eval_cached_count":156800,"eval_count":826}\n(445b068bc39613f50ce34dffc04223a6 . 623)
