ation watch closed clean. ext5 up 7742s with syslogd\n-R live, int2 up 8 minutes with it live, int3 still on its previous\nboot with the flag already set -- which is the better evidence, since\nit shows the flag persists across reboots rather than being injected\nper-boot. Seven of seven cron cameras boot into the sink. The\nstaircase mechanism is now closed at both ends: the reboots fire on\nschedule, every boot re-activates remote logging, and the splice\nsignature follows each one. Nothing left to watch in that class.\n\nOne transient to hold loosely: fleet-check read ext3 as 3/3 audio-dead\nat 07:01Z; sixty seconds later the same segments and the live RTSP\nstream were healthy. I reproduced nothing, the stream is fine, and I\nam deliberately not chasing it -- but if the 3/3-dead shape recurs\nwithout a real deafness behind it, that's a new false-alarm class in\nthe ear check and it will need a discriminator.\n\nThe sweep did not fire on its predicted ~07:05Z cadence, which\nconfirms again that .58's client is event-driven, not clocked. The\nARP logger is re-armed for two more hours. What I'd want next cycle:\nany recurrence logged with its trigger event, and -- if Nacho answers\n0062 -- this whole class either closes as someone's noisy app or\nopens into the question of what else that machine does.\n\nNext: sweep recurrence watch, relay 0060/0062 wait on Nacho, ext4\nstill his hands. The house is quiet in the way that means the\ninstruments are working.","filepath":"/root/personalization/audit/iar/aria/JOURNAL.org"}}}]},{"role":"tool","content":"[07:10:13] Success: Content appended to '/root/personalization/audit/iar/aria/JOURNAL.org'\n[t+25:45/60:00 c280/300]"},{"role":"assistant","content":null,"tool_calls":[{"id":"call_0kvwd56a","function":{"index":0,"name":"execute_code_local","arguments":{"command":"cd /root/personalization && git add -f audit/iar/aria/JOURNAL.org audit/iar/aria/HISTORY.log tasks/iar/aria/ROADMAP.org && git commit -q -m \"aria c289 close: j