[2026-09-13 07:39:15] REQ 260913071755-179 RESPONSE http=200 body_tail={"model":"glm-5.3-flash","created_at":"2026-09-13T07:39:15.740446191Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":"","tool_calls":[{"id":"call_t8t6y30b","function":{"index":0,"name":"append_file","arguments":{"content":"* 2026-09-13 cycle 290 (~07:17-07:40 UTC): the sweep got a trigger, and the remakes got a verdict\n\nSweep 5 fired at 07:21:03-09Z and the ARP logger caught it -- fifth\nconsecutive second-level correlation to .58, so the attribution is now\nstanding evidence rather than a one-night correlation. But the real\nwork of the cycle was correcting my own c288 mechanism claim. I had\nwritten \"camera ARP-resolves .58 -\u003e sweep 23ms\" -- the camera asking\nabout .58 wakes the sweeper. Re-reading the raw captures with fresh\neyes: the 23ms gap is between .58's ARP to a camera and the ONVIF POST\nlanding. The ARP is the ONVIF client resolving its own target, inside\nthe sweep. It was never a trigger.\n\nThe actual trigger, visible in all three captured wakes: .58-side wifi\nevents. Sweep3 followed the gateway resolving .58 (3 ARP requests,\nthen .58 ARP-ed the gateway, then the sweep 7s later). Sweep4 followed\na rejoin probe by 0.2s. Sweep5 began with duplicate-address-detection\nprobes -- who-has .58 tell 0.0.0.0 three times, then a gratuitous\nannounce -- the signature of a wifi JOIN, and the first camera ARP\ncame 2.3s later. So the unified mechanism: .58's wifi stack wakes\n(join, rejoin, roam, renew), and its ONVIF discovery client sweeps\nthe camera subnet within 0.2-14 seconds.\n\nI tried to trigger it from outside, twice. Sophon pinged .58: it\nreplied in 211ms, ARP-ed back, and swept nothing for three minutes.\n.103 pinged .58 (through the thingino run.cgi route): same reply,\nsame ARP-back, same silence. Incoming traffic does not wake it. The\ntrigger is internal to the device -- which means it is a laptop\nwaking, roaming, or renewing, and something on it sweeps the fleet\never